<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  prudent-server :: Prudent's Quarkus backend.

  IN THIS PHASE IT COMPILES AND NOTHING ELSE. There are no resources, no entities, no datasource
  and no migrations — those arrive in Phase 2, where there is something for them to do. What this
  module exists to prove today is that the dependency seam on the jZen framework resolves, which is
  the one thing that has to be true before anything else can be built on it.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    ============================================================================================
    THE EMPTY <relativePath/> IS THE DECISION, NOT A DETAIL. READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING IT.
    ============================================================================================

    An empty <relativePath/> tells Maven: do NOT look for this parent on the filesystem, resolve it
    from a repository like any other artifact. jZen's artifacts are unpublished
    (0.1.0-SNAPSHOT, no registry), so "a repository" means the LOCAL one, ~/.m2.

    THE PREREQUISITE MAVEN CANNOT EXPRESS. Something has to put the framework artifacts in the
    local repository first, and no POM can say so — a build cannot declare "go build another
    repository". That step is:

        task zen:framework:install

    which runs `./mvnw -B install -DskipTests` inside ../jZen/server.

    WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE WHEN YOU FORGET. Maven fails with

        Non-resolvable parent POM for prudent:prudent-server:0.1.0-SNAPSHOT:
        zen:zen-parent:pom:0.1.0-SNAPSHOT was not found

    which reads like a broken or malformed POM file. It is not. The POM is fine and the fix is to
    run `task zen:framework:install`. Run `task zen:info` first if that fails too — it reports
    whether the ../jZen checkout is even there.

    WHY NOT jZen ADR-026'S FILESYSTEM <relativePath>. jZen's own reference backend inherits this
    same parent with <relativePath>../../../server/pom.xml</relativePath>, and ADR-026 has a second
    product do the equivalent across repositories, under a shared parent folder with a root
    aggregator POM above both checkouts. Prudent does not, for two reasons:

      1. CLAUDE.md requires that all work happen inside this repository and that nothing reach
         outside the repo root. A root aggregator POM in the parent directory is a file above the
         repository root, created and maintained by neither repository.
      2. A cross-repository <relativePath> couples this build to a DIRECTORY LAYOUT rather than to
         a VERSION. The empty form couples it to a coordinate, which is what the eventual published
         dependency will also be — so publishing changes this block's <version> and deletes nothing
         else.

    This is a Prudent-owned concern, so Prudent's rule wins and the divergence is recorded:
    docs/DECISIONS.md ADR-001.

    THE COST, PLAINLY: there is no version boundary between the two repositories. They move in
    lockstep, and a `git pull` in ../jZen can break this build with no version to point at.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>zen</groupId>
    <artifactId>zen-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath/>
  </parent>

  <groupId>prudent</groupId>
  <artifactId>prudent-server</artifactId>
  <!--
    Declared, not inherited. It happens to match zen-parent's version string today, and inheriting
    it would silently make Prudent's version track the framework's — so that the day jZen releases
    0.2.0, Prudent's server is renumbered by a dependency. Prudent versions itself.
  -->
  <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  <name>Prudent :: server</name>
  <packaging>quarkus</packaging>

  <!--
    A single dependency-version override, taken here rather than reported upstream. task audit
    (docs/prudent-migration-plan.md Phase 5 §4) found io.netty:netty-codec-http:4.1.136.Final —
    pinned transitively by Quarkus 3.38.0's own BOM in zen-parent — vulnerable to GHSA-8c42-7qj2-3j46
    (CorsHandler overwriting an existing Vary header, enabling cache poisoning). Fixed in
    4.1.137.Final, which exists on Maven Central and is API-compatible (a patch release within the
    4.1.x line). This is the same "override a transitive dependency for a CVE" move admin/package.json
    already makes for react-router et al. — a routine child-POM override, not a jZen-side concern:
    the vulnerable class (io.netty.handler.codec.http.cors.CorsHandler) is not one Quarkus's own CORS
    handling routes through, but a patch release costs nothing and closes the finding outright rather
    than arguing the exploit path is unreachable in a suppressions file.
  -->
  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>io.netty</groupId>
        <artifactId>netty-codec-http</artifactId>
        <version>4.1.137.Final</version>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <dependencies>
    <!--
      The framework libraries. No <version> on any of them: zen-parent's dependencyManagement
      supplies it, which is half of what inheriting that parent buys (the other half is the Quarkus
      BOM, the Java release level, and plugin management).

      These three and no more. zen-identity, zen-email, zen-jobs and zen-ratelimit are real
      dependencies of the finished product and are deliberately absent here — a module that
      declares a dependency it does not use implies a surface that does not exist. They arrive in
      Phase 2 alongside the auth and persistence work that needs them.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-proto</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-transport</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      Auth, the `users` table every Prudent row's user_id references, and the UserRegistered CDI
      event Prudent observes to seed a new user's default categories and settings row. Not
      optional: without it there is no authenticated identity to scope a row to, and every
      ownership rule in this phase is stated in terms of one.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-identity</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      The abuse layer. Assembling it is the whole integration — the limits are production values
      by default (jZen ADR-029), so an application that configures nothing still gets protection.
      Its absence would be an open door on the login path of a personal-finance API, which is why
      it is here in the phase that first exposes one rather than in the phase that first tunes it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-ratelimit</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      ASSEMBLED IN PHASE 5, closing the obligation the comment below used to defer. zen-identity
      ships UserRetentionJob (the GDPR Art. 5(1)(e) dormancy-anonymisation cycle) as a plain
      callable with no scheduling of its own; zen-jobs is what gives it an external trigger — one
      that fires whether or not an instance happened to be awake under min-instances=0, which is
      what a cron inside the process could never do. PrudentRetentionCleanupJob (see
      prudent.server.retention) is the second job this dependency exists for: zen-identity's
      anonymisation has no application-observable event, so Prudent's own job sweeps for accounts
      it anonymised and removes the financial rows GDPR requires not to outlive them
      (docs/DECISIONS.md — the retention ADR this phase adds).
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-jobs</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      NO zen-email, and this absence is still not an oversight. Prudent sends no mail; zen-identity
      does not need it either — UserRegistered is fired as an event precisely so the framework
      stays free of any dependency on zen-email, and an application that wants no welcome message
      simply observes nothing (jZen ADR-007). This was checked against that class's contract
      rather than assumed.
    -->

    <!--
      The runtime for the DTOs generated from ../proto below. Declared explicitly, with no
      <version> (zen-parent manages it at ${protobuf.version}), even though zen-proto already
      drags it in transitively: this module compiles code that calls protobuf-java directly, and
      a direct compile dependency that is only satisfied by accident disappears the day the
      module it travelled through drops it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.google.protobuf</groupId>
      <artifactId>protobuf-java</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-arc</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-rest</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      NO quarkus-rest-jackson. NOT AT ANY PRIORITY. NOT LATER.

      jZen's JSON is canonical proto3 JSON produced by protobuf's JsonFormat, written by
      zen-transport's own MessageBodyWriter. quarkus-rest-jackson registers itself for
      application/json through a BUILD-TIME path that does not consult writer priority, so merely
      out-ranking it does not work: it wins, tries to reflect over a protobuf-generated class, and
      returns 500 on every proto response body. The extension must be ABSENT.

      This is on the list of rules that fail late and confusingly rather than at the point of the
      mistake, which is why it is written here rather than assumed.
    -->

    <!--
      PERSISTENCE. Panache in ACTIVE-RECORD style: entities extend PanacheEntityBase and there are
      no repository classes on this server. (The repository pattern lives on the CLIENT, over
      ZenClient — Phase 3, a different thing wearing the same word.)
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-hibernate-orm-panache</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-jdbc-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      THE SINGLE MIGRATION AUTHORITY. Never two migration systems on one database. zen-identity
      ships its migrations to the same db/migration classpath location and they run into THIS
      database, which is why Prudent's versions are UTC timestamps rather than a reserved band
      (docs/DECISIONS.md ADR-002).
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-flyway</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      Bean Validation on the request messages. The validation this phase owes is server-side and
      unconditional: an id from a client is not identity, and a currency or icon_key from a client
      is not a fact.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-hibernate-validator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Entity <-> proto mapping, so a resource method only ever names the domain model. No
      <version>: zen-parent manages it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.mapstruct</groupId>
      <artifactId>mapstruct</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.rest-assured</groupId>
      <artifactId>rest-assured</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      Mints a SecurityIdentity in a test without a live Supabase, which is how every ownership
      assertion in this phase is made: the user-scoping suite needs TWO identities that differ,
      and @TestSecurity is the mechanism zen_demo's own AdminUserResourceTest uses.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-test-security</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!--
        Declared with NO <executions>, deliberately, and this module is <packaging>quarkus</packaging>
        while zen-parent registers the plugin with <extensions>true</extensions> — so that packaging's
        own lifecycle already binds generate-code, generate-code-tests and build to their phases.

        The Quarkus archetype's explicit <execution> listing those three goals is correct for a
        `jar` module and a DUPLICATE here: it binds `build` a second time, so a native build
        compiles the image TWICE and the second overwrites the first. Both builds succeed and the
        output is identical, so the only symptom is minutes of wall clock on every deploy.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>${quarkus.platform.group-id}</groupId>
        <artifactId>quarkus-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        MapStruct's annotation processor, which generates the mapper implementations at compile
        time. ${mapstruct.version} comes from zen-parent, the same property zen-identity's own
        mapper build uses.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <annotationProcessorPaths>
            <path>
              <groupId>org.mapstruct</groupId>
              <artifactId>mapstruct-processor</artifactId>
              <version>${mapstruct.version}</version>
            </path>
          </annotationProcessorPaths>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        NO jandex-maven-plugin HERE, AND THE REASON MATTERS FOR WHOEVER ADDS THE SECOND MODULE.

        The rule is that every module contributing CDI beans or JAX-RS providers must produce a
        META-INF/jandex.idx, because without one Quarkus never discovers them — no error, the
        beans simply do nothing. It does not apply to this module: prudent-server is the
        APPLICATION module (<packaging>quarkus</packaging>), and Quarkus indexes the application's
        own classes automatically. The rule is about LIBRARY jars that arrive as dependencies,
        which is why zen-identity and zen-transport both run it and this module does not.

        THE MOMENT PRUDENT SPLITS OUT A LIBRARY MODULE — anything this POM would list as a
        <dependency> that carries an @Entity, a @Mapper, a JAX-RS provider or any CDI bean — that
        module runs jandex-maven-plugin. Stated here rather than left ambiguous, because the
        failure is silent and the symptom appears in the application rather than in the library.
      -->

      <!--
        THE JAVA HALF OF THE CONTRACT. ../proto/prudent/v1/*.proto is canonical; these DTOs are
        derived, and a derived file is never hand-edited.

        NOT TRACKED, ON PURPOSE. The output lands in target/generated-sources/protobuf, which is
        gitignored, because this plugin RESOLVES THE protoc BINARY FROM MAVEN CENTRAL — anyone
        with the Maven wrapper regenerates it hermetically, so committing it would only create a
        second copy to keep honest. The Dart half is the opposite (tracked), for the opposite
        reason: it needs a system protoc that a Flutter developer has no reason to install. See
        ../proto/README.md and docs/DECISIONS.md ADR-006.

        NO <version>: zen-parent's pluginManagement supplies it, which is part of what inheriting
        that parent buys.

        NO ../jZen/proto ON THE IMPORT PATH, deliberately. Prudent's protos import nothing from
        zen.v1 — the framework's ZenError is an error *body*, returned in place of a response
        rather than embedded in one, which is why none of jZen's own protos import common.proto
        either. Adding the include path would be configuration nothing uses, and it would suggest
        an import is available when the Dart half of the loop cannot follow one across
        repositories (../proto/README.md records what was measured).
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>io.github.ascopes</groupId>
        <artifactId>protobuf-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <protocVersion>${protobuf.version}</protocVersion>
          <sourceDirectories>
            <sourceDirectory>${project.basedir}/../proto</sourceDirectory>
          </sourceDirectories>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <goals>
              <goal>generate</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        ONE RULE: `UnnecessaryFullyQualifiedName` (pmd-ruleset.xml), flagging code like
        `prudent.server.account.AccountBalance("PLN", 100_00L)` written inline instead of
        `import prudent.server.account.AccountBalance;` plus the simple name.

        BOUND TO `test-compile`, WITH `failOnViolation=true` — a violation turns the build red, not
        a warning line nobody reads. Not `compile`: a fully qualified reference compiles fine, so
        nothing about compilation would ever catch this on its own; the rule needs to run somewhere
        `task test:server` (`./mvnw -B test`) actually reaches, and `test-compile` is the last phase
        before `test` that both `mvn test` and `mvn package`/`verify` pass through — so the same
        check covers `task test:server`, the contract loop's `generate:api:schema`
        (`mvn package`), and a native build alike, rather than only an explicit `mvn verify`.

        `includeTests=true` because the one violation this rule found on introduction
        (server/src/test/java/prudent/server/UserScopingTest.java) was in a test class — the
        default scans main sources only, which would have missed it.

        `skipEmptyReport=false` — an empty report is a legitimate outcome (no violations) and PMD
        skipping its own summary in that case has caused "did this even run?" confusion elsewhere;
        cheap to pin here rather than rediscover.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-pmd-plugin</artifactId>
        <!--
          NO <version> HERE ANY MORE — CONSUMED FROM jZen (issue #61, fixed upstream in PR #62,
          jZen commit 9425e9a, inside this repository's pinned JZEN_REF). zen-parent's own
          pluginManagement now supplies the version via ${maven-pmd-plugin.version} (3.27.0),
          pluginManagement-only exactly as this repository proposed upstream — the version is the
          framework's concern now, the ruleset and the execution below stay this module's own,
          since each consuming module opts in rather than every inheriting module being linted
          unconditionally. The reasoning for WHY 3.27.0 (the default PMD 6.55.0's bundled ASM
          cannot parse a Java 25 class file — "Unsupported class file major version 69" — and the
          plugin still reports BUILD SUCCESS having checked nothing) now lives in zen-parent's own
          pom.xml, next to the property it explains, rather than duplicated here.
        -->
        <configuration>
          <rulesets>
            <ruleset>${project.basedir}/pmd-ruleset.xml</ruleset>
          </rulesets>
          <includeTests>true</includeTests>
          <linkXRef>false</linkXRef>
          <skipEmptyReport>false</skipEmptyReport>
          <!--
            EXCLUDES two generated-sources roots, both otherwise scanned: this plugin walks every
            compile source root, and generator plugins add theirs to that list. `protobuf`
            (protobuf-maven-plugin's output — never hand-edited, ../proto/README.md,
            docs/DECISIONS.md ADR-006) and `annotations` (MapStruct's *MapperImpl classes — also
            never hand-edited, see e.g. AccountMapper's own javadoc on why only entity->proto is
            written by hand). A style rule about how code is WRITTEN has nothing to check in code
            nobody writes; PMD 7 parses both directories fine and genuinely flags qualifiers in the
            generated mapper impls ("'AccountMapper': 'uuidToString' is already in scope") that
            MapStruct itself chose to write fully qualified — a real finding about generated code,
            not a bug in this rule, and not this rule's job to fix.
          -->
          <excludeRoots>
            <excludeRoot>${project.build.directory}/generated-sources/protobuf</excludeRoot>
            <excludeRoot>${project.build.directory}/generated-sources/annotations</excludeRoot>
          </excludeRoots>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <phase>test-compile</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>check</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <failOnViolation>true</failOnViolation>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <profiles>
    <!--
      OpenAPI is BUILD TOOLING, and the profile is what makes that true rather than merely said.
      Prudent has no resources yet, so the extension documents nothing today; the profile is here
      now so that Phase 2's first resource lands in a module already shaped correctly, rather than
      shipping the extension to production once and being retrofitted.

      quarkus-smallrye-openapi exists to produce one file — the merged openapi.json the contract
      loop consumes. Shipping it additionally serves /openapi to the internet for the life of every
      deployed revision: a complete, current map of every route, verb, parameter and status code
      the service accepts, published to anyone who asks. Nothing needs it there.

      WHY A PROFILE AND NOT quarkus.smallrye-openapi.enable=false. That stops the endpoint and
      still bakes the extension and its scanner into the image, so the attack-surface argument
      survives and the cold-start one is untouched. Removing the dependency removes the code.

      WHY THE ACTIVATION IS INVERTED. It is ON unless -Dnative is passed, so every ordinary build,
      test run and contract regeneration has the extension and only the native production image
      lacks it. Getting this backwards is expensive and quiet: the contract task would package
      cleanly, write no openapi.json, and the downstream generator would then SKIP on its
      "does openapi.json exist?" guard — leaving generated types stale while the gate reported
      success.
    -->
    <profile>
      <id>openapi</id>
      <activation>
        <property><name>!native</name></property>
      </activation>
      <dependencies>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
          <artifactId>quarkus-smallrye-openapi</artifactId>
        </dependency>
      </dependencies>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
